===Etymology 2===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*bʰeh₂-|id=speak}}
Possibly from dialectal {{m|en|bene}}, {{m|en|been}}, {{m|en|bean||help given by neighbours}}, from {{der|en|enm|been}}, {{m|enm|bene||neighbourly help, prayer, petition, request, extra service given by a tenant to his lord}},<ref>{{cite-web |title=Archived copy |titleurl=http://education.yahoo.com/reference/dictionary/entry/bee |accessdate=4 March 2012 |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20120616030024/http://education.yahoo.com/reference/dictionary/entry/bee |archivedate=16 June 2012 }}</ref><ref>http://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/bee%5B3%5D</ref> from {{der|en|ang|bēn||prayer, request, petition, favour, compulsory service}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*bōniz||prayer, request, supplication}}. Thus a variant of obsolete {{m|en|ben||prayer; petition}} and {{doublet|en|boon|nocap=1}}. Cognate with {{cog|da|bøn||prayer}}, {{cog|nl|ban||curse}}, {{cog|de|Bann||ban}}. More at {{l|en|ban}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|contest}} A [[contest]], especially for [[spelling]]; see {{m|en|spelling bee}}.
#: {{co|en|geography '''bee'''}}
# {{senseid|en|gathering}} A community [[gathering]] to share labour, e.g. a sewing bee or a quilting bee.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1856|author=Samuel Griswold Goodrich|title=Recollections of a Lifetime
|passage=The cellar {{...}} was dug by a '''''bee''''' in a single day.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1973|author=Alan Skeoch; Tony H. Smith|title=Canadians and their society|page=139
|passage=There was but little variation in types of buildings in the pioneer period: house, church, store, barn and mill were usually much alike except in size, and a raising '''bee''' was the ordinary means of their erection.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Tim Blanning|title=The reinvention of the night|journal=Times Literary Supplement|date=21 Sep 2011
|passage=Particularly resistant, for example, in many parts of northern Europe was the “spinning '''bee'''”, a nocturnal gathering of women to exchange gossip, stories, refreshment and – crucially – light and heat, as they spun wool or flax, knitted or sewed.}}

